ORGO.reg({"chapters":{"rings-1":{"id":"rings-1","n":1,"title":"Rings and ring strain","lecture":"2026-10-20","from":"Lecture notes 10/20, pages 1 to 3","goals":["Name the two parts of ring strain and say where each one comes from","Count the eclipsed C-H pairs in a flat ring of three, four, five or six carbons","Explain why cyclobutane and cyclopentane bend out of the plane, and what each one gives up to do it","Explain why the chair of cyclohexane has no angle strain and no torsional strain","Draw a chair with all twelve hydrogens using the parallel-line rule"],"sections":[{"id":"cyclopropane-angle","title":"Cyclopropane: 60° angles, angle strain and bent bonds","pages":["L1020:1"],"html":"<p>Chapter 5 is about rings, the <b>cycloalkanes</b>. The lecture starts with a comparison. In propane the middle carbon is free to take the angle an {{sp3}} carbon prefers, so the C-C-C angle is 109.5°. Close three carbons into a ring and geometry takes over: a triangle has internal angles of 60°.</p>\n[[mol:CCC]]\n[[mol:C1CC1]]\n<p>Forcing a bond angle away from 109.5° raises the energy of the molecule. That extra energy is <b>angle strain</b>, and cyclopropane has a great deal of it, because each angle is squeezed by almost 50°.</p>\n<p>The orbital picture shows the cost. The {{sp3}} orbitals cannot point along the lines between the carbons, because those lines are only 60° apart. The orbitals point outside the triangle instead and overlap <b>off-axis</b>, which gives curved, <b>bent bonds</b>. Learn the chain of reasoning as the professor wrote it: bent bond = less overlap = <b>weaker bond</b>.</p>\n<p>The numbers to know: the C-C bond strength is <b>88 kcal/mol in propane</b> and only <b>65 kcal/mol in cyclopropane</b>.</p>","micro":[{"t":"How bent is a bent bond?","h":"<p>A sigma bond is strongest when the two orbitals point straight at each other, so that the shared electrons sit on the line between the two nuclei. In cyclopropane the orbitals that make each C-C bond are about 104° apart at each carbon, not 60°. The electron density of every ring bond therefore bulges outward, away from the line that joins the nuclei. Chemists sometimes call these banana bonds because of the curve. The carbons are 60° apart, the orbitals are not, and the mismatch is the lost overlap.</p>"},{"t":"Reading the two bond strengths","h":"<p>A bond strength is the energy needed to break the bond. Breaking a C-C bond in propane costs 88 kcal/mol. Breaking one in cyclopropane costs 65 kcal/mol, which is 23 kcal/mol less. The bond is easier to break for two linked reasons: it was a poorer bond to begin with (less overlap), and breaking it opens the ring, which lets the carbons relax back toward 109.5°. A common mistake is to read a smaller number as a more stable molecule. Here the smaller number means a weaker bond and a more reactive ring.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"Strained rings open easily, and the past exam uses it","h":"<p>A three-membered ring is a loaded spring. On the past exam, Question 4B, methoxide opens an <b>epoxide</b>, a three-membered ring that contains an oxygen and has the same 60° angles. An alkoxide is normally a poor leaving group, yet the ring C-O bond breaks in an {{SN2}} step because opening the ring releases the strain. When a mechanism question contains a three-membered or four-membered ring, ask first whether relief of ring strain is what drives the reaction.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Why is the C-C bond of cyclopropane (65 kcal/mol) weaker than the C-C bond of propane (88 kcal/mol)?","a":"The ring forces the carbons 60° apart, but the sp3 orbitals cannot bend that far, so they overlap off the line between the nuclei. Less overlap means a weaker bond. Breaking the bond also releases the angle strain, which makes it cheaper still."},"check":["ring-strain-007"]},{"id":"cyclopropane-torsional","title":"Torsional strain: six eclipsed C-H pairs, and the definition of ring strain","pages":["L1020:1"],"html":"<p>Angle strain is only half of the problem. The lecture draws cyclopropane with all six hydrogens, then looks straight down the C1-C2 bond in a <b>Newman projection</b>. The third carbon, C3, is attached to both the front carbon and the back carbon, so it bridges the two.</p>\n[[newman:H,H,CH2|H,H,CH2|0]]\n<p>The picture shows that every C-H bond on the front carbon lines up exactly with a C-H bond on the back carbon. The bonds are <b>eclipsed</b>. Eclipsed bonds raise the energy, exactly as they do in the eclipsed conformation of ethane, and that energy is <b>torsional strain</b>.</p>\n<p>Count them. Each of the three C-C bonds has two eclipsed C-H/C-H pairs, one on the top face of the ring and one on the bottom face. That makes <b>6 C-H/C-H eclipsing interactions</b> in cyclopropane.</p>\n<p>The lecture closes the page with a summary box, which is the definition to write on an exam:</p>\n<p><b>Ring strain:</b></p>\n<ol><li><b>angle strain</b> (bond angles forced away from 109.5°)</li><li><b>torsional strain</b> (eclipsed bonds on neighbouring atoms)</li></ol>\n<p>Every ring in the rest of this lecture is analysed with these two questions, in this order.</p>","micro":[{"t":"A quick way to count eclipsing interactions","h":"<p>In any flat ring, each C-C bond has one eclipsed pair of C-H bonds above the ring and one below it. A flat ring of n carbons has n ring bonds, so it has 2n eclipsed C-H/C-H pairs: 6 for cyclopropane, 8 for flat cyclobutane, 10 for flat cyclopentane and 12 for flat cyclohexane. These are exactly the four numbers in the lecture. For scale, the three eclipsed pairs of ethane cost about 3 kcal/mol in total, so each eclipsed C-H/C-H pair costs about 1 kcal/mol.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"The Newman projection comes back three more times","h":"<p>The Newman projection is the working tool of this whole unit. Here it shows eclipsed bonds in a small ring. In the 10/27 lecture it shows why an axial methyl group costs energy (a gauche interaction with the ring). In the 11/12 lecture it shows the anti-periplanar hydrogen that an E2 reaction needs, which is what the past exam tests in Questions 3D and 5B. If looking down a bond and drawing what is in front and what is behind feels slow, practise it now on cyclopropane, where the answer is simple.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Cyclobutane and cyclopentane escape some torsional strain by bending out of the plane. Why can cyclopropane not do the same?","a":"Three points always lie in one plane, so a three-membered ring has no way to pucker. Its carbons are locked flat, and the C-H bonds stay fully eclipsed. Cyclopropane has to carry all of its angle strain and all of its torsional strain."},"check":["ring-strain-005"]},{"id":"cyclobutane","title":"Cyclobutane: 90° if flat, so it puckers to 88°","pages":["L1020:2"],"html":"<p>The same two questions for a four-membered ring. The open-chain comparison is butane, with angles of 109.5°.</p>\n[[mol:C1CCC1]]\n<p>Imagine cyclobutane as a flat square:</p>\n<ol><li><b>Angle strain:</b> 109.5° is forced down to <b>90°</b>. That is less of a squeeze than in cyclopropane, but still a large one.</li><li><b>Torsional strain:</b> looking down any ring bond, the C-H bonds are eclipsed. A flat square has <b>8 eclipsing C-H/C-H interactions</b>.</li></ol>\n<p>Real cyclobutane is not flat. It adopts a <b>puckered</b> conformation: the ring folds slightly along a diagonal, like a square of paper bent across its corners, and the C-C-C angle becomes <b>88°</b>.</p>\n<p>Why would the molecule make its angles 2° worse? Because folding the ring twists each C-C bond a little, so the C-H bonds on neighbouring carbons are no longer exactly eclipsed. In the professor's words, the puckered conformation <b>relieves some torsional strain</b>. The molecule pays a small extra price in angle strain and gets back a larger saving in torsional strain.</p>\n<p>The word to keep is <b>some</b>. Cyclobutane is still a highly strained ring.</p>","micro":[{"t":"Why puckering always makes the angles smaller","h":"<p>A flat square has the largest internal angles a four-membered ring can have, exactly 90°. Any fold out of the plane brings the corners closer together and makes the angles smaller, which is why the puckered ring has 88° and not something nearer to 109.5°. So puckering can never help angle strain in cyclobutane. It helps only torsional strain. The ring settles at the fold where the next bit of torsional relief would cost more in angle strain than it saves. This is a balance between two kinds of strain, and the same balance decides the shape of cyclopentane.</p>"},{"t":"How strained is it, in numbers?","h":"<p>The heats of formation in the 10/27 lecture (Table 5.1) let you put a number on ring strain. A strain-free {{CH2}} group has a heat of formation of −4.9 kcal/mol. Four of them would give −19.6 kcal/mol, but cyclobutane measures +6.8 kcal/mol. The difference, about 26 kcal/mol, is the ring strain. The same sum for cyclopropane gives about 27 kcal/mol. The two rings carry almost the same total strain, although cyclobutane spreads it over four carbons instead of three.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"Four-membered rings in medicine and on the exam","h":"<p>Penicillin works because of a strained four-membered ring. Its <b>β-lactam</b> is a cyclic amide in a four-membered ring. An ordinary amide is unreactive, but ring strain makes this one easy to open, and it reacts with a serine in the bacterial enzyme that cross-links the cell wall, which shuts the enzyme down. On the past exam, Question 9, a carbocation forms next to a four-membered ring, and a ring bond shifts so that the ring expands to five carbons. The driving force is the relief of the strain described in this section. The 11/10 homework uses the same idea.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Puckering takes the angle of cyclobutane from 90° to 88°, further from 109.5°. Why does the molecule pucker anyway?","a":"Folding the ring moves the C-H bonds on neighbouring carbons away from the fully eclipsed arrangement. The torsional strain it relieves is larger than the small amount of angle strain it adds, so the puckered shape is lower in energy overall."},"check":["ring-strain-008"]},{"id":"cyclopentane","title":"Cyclopentane: almost no angle strain, so torsional strain decides the shape","pages":["L1020:2"],"html":"<p>Pentane has angles of 109.5°. A flat, regular pentagon has internal angles of <b>108°</b>. That is only 1.5° away from ideal, so flat cyclopentane would have <b>very little angle strain</b>.</p>\n[[mol:C1CCCC1]]\n<p>If angle strain were the whole story, cyclopentane would be flat. It is not, because of the second question. In a flat five-membered ring every C-H bond is eclipsed with its neighbours: <b>10 C-H/C-H eclipsing interactions if flat</b>. That is more than in either of the smaller rings.</p>\n<p>So the ring bends out of the plane. The lecture draws <b>two energy-minimum conformations</b>:</p>\n<ul><li>the <b>envelope</b>: four carbons in one plane and the fifth lifted out of it, like the flap of an envelope;</li><li>the <b>half-chair</b>: three carbons in a plane, with one neighbour above the plane and the other below it.</li></ul>\n<p>The sentence written next to them is the one to remember: <b>lots of torsional strain relieved, at the expense of angle strain</b>. Bending the ring makes the angles a little smaller than 108°, which costs a little, and staggers many of the C-H bonds, which saves much more.</p>\n<p>Compare this with cyclobutane. There, puckering relieves only some torsional strain. Here it relieves lots of it, which is why cyclopentane is far less strained than the three-membered and four-membered rings.</p>","micro":[{"t":"The pucker does not stay in one place","h":"<p>In the envelope, one carbon is the flap. Nothing makes that carbon special: any of the five can be the flap, and the molecule passes from one envelope to the next through half-chair shapes with almost no energy barrier. The out-of-plane bump travels around the ring, and at room temperature it does so extremely fast. This is why the lecture draws two conformations of similar energy and does not name one as the single shape of cyclopentane. Using the heats of formation from the 10/27 lecture, the total strain that remains is only about 6 kcal/mol, compared with about 26 to 27 kcal/mol for cyclobutane and cyclopropane.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"The five-membered ring in DNA and RNA","h":"<p>The sugars of DNA and RNA, deoxyribose and ribose, are five-membered rings (four carbons and an oxygen), and they are puckered into envelope shapes for the reason given here. Which atom forms the flap is different in the two nucleic acids, and that difference is part of why the DNA double helix (B form) and double-stranded RNA (A form) have different shapes. Within this course, five-membered rings also appear as the comfortable product of ring expansion: on the past exam, Question 9, a strained four-membered ring becomes a five-membered ring.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Flat cyclopentane would have nearly perfect 108° angles. Why is cyclopentane not flat?","a":"A flat ring would have 10 eclipsed C-H/C-H pairs, which is a lot of torsional strain. Bending into an envelope or half-chair staggers many of those bonds. The small angle strain this adds is much less than the torsional strain it removes."},"check":["ring-strain-009"]},{"id":"cyclohexane-chair","title":"Cyclohexane: flat would be 120° and fully eclipsed, the chair is strain-free","pages":["L1020:3"],"html":"<p>For cyclohexane the flat ring has a new kind of problem. A flat hexagon has internal angles of <b>120°</b>. This time the angle is too large: the bonds would be spread open from 109.5° to 120°. That is still angle strain, because strain is any departure from 109.5°, in either direction. A flat ring would also have <b>12 C-H/C-H eclipsing interactions</b>, the most of any ring in this lecture.</p>\n<p>Real cyclohexane avoids both. It takes up the <b>chair</b> conformation:</p>\n[[chair:|A|H]]\n<p>In the chair there is <b>no torsional strain and no angle strain</b>. The C-C-C angle is <b>111.5°</b> (the lecture's value, which is the one to use; tables usually print 111.4°).</p>\n<p>The whole lecture fits in one table:</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ring</th><th>Angle if flat</th><th>Eclipsed C-H pairs if flat</th><th>What the real molecule does</th></tr><tr><td>cyclopropane</td><td>60°</td><td>6</td><td>stays flat: bent bonds, all strain kept</td></tr><tr><td>cyclobutane</td><td>90°</td><td>8</td><td>puckers (88°): some torsional strain relieved</td></tr><tr><td>cyclopentane</td><td>108°</td><td>10</td><td>envelope and half-chair: lots of torsional strain relieved, at the expense of angle strain</td></tr><tr><td>cyclohexane</td><td>120°</td><td>12</td><td>chair (111.5°): no angle strain, no torsional strain</td></tr></table>","micro":[{"t":"Is 111.5° really free of angle strain?","h":"<p>The chair angle is 2° wider than 109.5°, so a careful reader may ask why the lecture says no angle strain. Propane itself has a C-C-C angle slightly wider than 109.5°, because two carbon groups take more room than two hydrogens. So 111.5° is the relaxed angle for a {{CH2}} between two carbons, not a strained one. The measurement agrees: in the 10/27 lecture the heat of formation per {{CH2}} of cyclohexane is −4.9 kcal/mol, exactly the value for a {{CH2}} in a long strain-free chain.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"The most important shape in the unit","h":"<p>Six-membered rings are everywhere because the chair costs nothing. Glucose exists mainly as a six-membered ring in a chair, with its groups in the roomy positions around the edge. Steroids such as cholesterol are built from three fused six-membered rings and one five-membered ring. On the past exam the chair carries more points than any other drawing: Question 4A (cis or trans, most stable chair), Question 8 (draw both chairs and relate them), and Questions 3D and 5B (which hydrogen can leave in an E2). All of them start from the chair on this page.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"How does one change of shape, from flat hexagon to chair, remove both kinds of strain at once?","a":"Moving alternate carbons up and down out of the plane closes the ring angles from 120° to about 111.5°, which is the relaxed angle for a tetrahedral carbon in a chain. The same motion twists every C-C bond by 60°, so that all the C-H bonds on neighbouring carbons become staggered."},"check":["ring-strain-006"]},{"id":"reading-the-chair","title":"Reading and drawing the chair: parallel lines, tetrahedral carbons, staggered bonds","pages":["L1020:3"],"html":"<p>The lecture draws the chair with all 12 hydrogens, in three colours, with one instruction: <b>lines of the same colour are parallel to each other</b>. This rule is how a correct chair is drawn.</p>\n<ul><li>The six ring bonds come in three parallel pairs. Each ring bond is parallel to the ring bond directly across the ring from it.</li><li>Six C-H bonds are vertical. They alternate up, down, up, down around the ring.</li><li>The other six C-H bonds point outward around the middle of the ring. Each one is parallel to the ring bonds <b>one bond away</b> from it, which means the two ring bonds that do not touch its carbon but touch that carbon's neighbours.</li></ul>\n<p>Two facts are written under the drawing, and they are the reason the chair is strain-free:</p>\n<ol><li><b>All C's are tetrahedral.</b> Every carbon has its four bonds at close to 109.5°: no angle strain.</li><li><b>All C-H bonds on adjacent carbons are staggered.</b> No bond is eclipsed: no torsional strain.</li></ol>\n<p>A Newman projection down any ring bond of the chair shows the second fact:</p>\n[[newman:H,CH2,H|CH2,H,H|60]]\n<p>Compare this with the eclipsed projection of cyclopropane. Here every bond on the front carbon sits in a gap between bonds on the back carbon. The next lecture gives the vertical hydrogens and the outward hydrogens their names: axial and equatorial.</p>","micro":[{"t":"A drawing recipe that always works","h":"<p>Draw two parallel lines that slope gently and are offset from each other. Join their ends with a V on the left that points down and a V on the right that points up, keeping each new line parallel to the one across the ring. You now have three pairs of parallel ring bonds. Add the vertical bonds: up on every carbon that sits at a peak of the ring, down on every carbon that sits in a dip. Then add the outward bond on each carbon, slanted slightly the opposite way from its vertical bond (down a little where the vertical bond is up), and parallel to the ring bonds one bond away. If two outward bonds on the same side of the drawing are not parallel to ring bonds, redraw them.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"Build it once with your hands","h":"<p>The molecular model set is allowed in the exam room, and chairs are where it earns its place. Build cyclohexane, set it in the chair, and look down one C-C bond: you will see the staggered Newman projection above without having to imagine it. Then check the parallel rule against the model. The past exam asks for chairs in Questions 3D, 4A, 5B and 8, and in each of them a sloppy chair, with bonds that are not parallel, makes it hard to tell which groups point up and which point down. A clean drawing is part of getting the chemistry right.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Why are all the C-H bonds staggered in the chair but eclipsed in a flat ring?","a":"In a flat ring the four ring carbons around any bond lie in one plane, a dihedral angle of 0°, which lines up the C-H bonds on neighbouring carbons. In the chair the ring carbons around each bond are twisted about 60° from each other, so each C-H bond on one carbon sits between two bonds on the next carbon."},"check":[]}],"homework":[{"q":"HW: build a model. (Cyclopentane: the two energy-minimum conformations.)","from":"L1020:2","steps":[{"t":"Build the ring flat first","h":"Join five carbons into a ring, add two hydrogens to each, and press the ring flat on the table. Look down any C-C bond. The C-H bonds on the front carbon hide the C-H bonds on the back carbon: they are eclipsed. Count around the ring: 5 ring bonds, 2 eclipsed pairs on each, 10 eclipsing C-H/C-H interactions. The ring angles are 108°, so the model is not fighting you on angles."},{"t":"Make the envelope","h":"Hold four carbons flat on the table and lift the fifth. Look down the two bonds that lead to the lifted carbon: the C-H bonds there are now staggered or close to it. Along the bond on the far side of the ring, between the two carbons opposite the flap, the C-H bonds are still eclipsed. Torsional strain is relieved, but not all of it."},{"t":"Make the half-chair","h":"Hold three neighbouring carbons flat. Push one of the remaining two above that plane and the other below it. The ring is now twisted, and the remaining eclipsing is spread around the ring instead of being concentrated on one bond."},{"t":"Notice what it cost","h":"In both shapes the ring angles are slightly smaller than 108°: you can feel the model resist a little. That is the meaning of the lecture's sentence: lots of torsional strain relieved, at the expense of angle strain."},{"t":"Move the flap","h":"Push the flap down and lift its neighbour. The model passes through a half-chair and becomes a new envelope with almost no effort. The real molecule does this constantly."}],"answer":"Flat cyclopentane has 108° angles (very little angle strain) but 10 eclipsed C-H/C-H pairs. The envelope (four carbons in a plane, one out) and the half-chair (three in a plane, one above, one below) stagger many of those bonds. Both are energy minima: lots of torsional strain is relieved at the expense of a little angle strain."}],"summary":["Ring strain has two parts: angle strain (angles forced away from 109.5°) and torsional strain (eclipsed bonds).","Cyclopropane has 60° angles, bent bonds with poor overlap (C-C 65 kcal/mol against 88 in propane) and 6 eclipsed C-H pairs, and it cannot pucker.","Flat cyclobutane would have 90° angles and 8 eclipsed C-H pairs, so the real ring puckers to 88°, which relieves some torsional strain.","Flat cyclopentane would have 108° angles, very little angle strain, but 10 eclipsed C-H pairs, so it adopts the envelope and half-chair, relieving lots of torsional strain at the expense of angle strain.","Flat cyclohexane would have 120° angles and 12 eclipsed C-H pairs.","The chair of cyclohexane has no angle strain and no torsional strain: C-C-C is 111.5°, every carbon is tetrahedral and every C-H bond is staggered.","In a chair drawing, lines that should be parallel must be drawn parallel: ring bonds in three pairs, and each outward C-H parallel to the ring bonds one bond away."]}},"mols":{"CCC":{"w":64,"h":18,"p":[[0,"M 9.2,15.2 L 31.7,2.2",0],[1,"M 31.7,2.2 L 54.2,15.2",0],[-1,"M 30.6,2.8 L 31.7,2.2 L 32.8,2.8",0]],"t":[],"a":[[9.2,15.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,2.2,"C",0,0,2,0],[54.2,15.2,"C",0,0,3,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1]]},"C1CC1":{"w":32,"h":37,"p":[[0,"M 27.8,18.4 L 3.8,4.6",0],[1,"M 3.8,4.6 L 3.8,32.3",0],[2,"M 3.8,32.3 L 27.8,18.4",0],[-1,"M 26.6,17.7 L 27.8,18.4 L 26.6,19.1",0],[-1,"M 5.0,5.3 L 3.8,4.6 L 3.8,6.0",0],[-1,"M 3.8,30.9 L 3.8,32.3 L 5.0,31.6",0]],"t":[],"a":[[27.8,18.4,"C",0,0,2,0],[3.8,4.6,"C",0,0,2,0],[3.8,32.3,"C",0,0,2,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[2,0,1]]},"C1CCC1":{"w":52,"h":52,"p":[[0,"M 45.2,25.7 L 25.7,6.2",0],[1,"M 25.7,6.2 L 6.2,25.7",0],[2,"M 6.2,25.7 L 25.7,45.2",0],[3,"M 25.7,45.2 L 45.2,25.7",0],[-1,"M 44.3,24.8 L 45.2,25.7 L 44.3,26.7",0],[-1,"M 26.7,7.2 L 25.7,6.2 L 24.8,7.2",0],[-1,"M 7.2,24.8 L 6.2,25.7 L 7.2,26.7",0],[-1,"M 24.8,44.3 L 25.7,45.2 L 26.7,44.3",0]],"t":[],"a":[[45.2,25.7,"C",0,0,2,0],[25.7,6.2,"C",0,0,2,0],[6.2,25.7,"C",0,0,2,0],[25.7,45.2,"C",0,0,2,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[2,3,1],[3,0,1]]},"C1CCCC1":{"w":57,"h":60,"p":[[0,"M 49.7,29.7 L 33.4,7.2",0],[1,"M 33.4,7.2 L 6.9,15.8",0],[2,"M 6.9,15.8 L 6.9,43.6",0],[3,"M 6.9,43.6 L 33.4,52.2",0],[4,"M 33.4,52.2 L 49.7,29.7",0],[-1,"M 48.9,28.6 L 49.7,29.7 L 48.9,30.8",0],[-1,"M 34.2,8.3 L 33.4,7.2 L 32.1,7.6",0],[-1,"M 8.3,15.4 L 6.9,15.8 L 6.9,17.2",0],[-1,"M 6.9,42.2 L 6.9,43.6 L 8.3,44.0",0],[-1,"M 32.1,51.8 L 33.4,52.2 L 34.2,51.1",0]],"t":[],"a":[[49.7,29.7,"C",0,0,2,0],[33.4,7.2,"C",0,0,2,0],[6.9,15.8,"C",0,0,2,0],[6.9,43.6,"C",0,0,2,0],[33.4,52.2,"C",0,0,2,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[2,3,1],[3,4,1],[4,0,1]]}}});
